### What are the four Coffman conditions?

Edward Coffman identified in 1971 that four conditions must all hold simultaneously for a deadlock to occur. If any single condition is broken, deadlock is impossible.

### How do you break mutual exclusion?

Mutual exclusion is inherent to locks: only one thread can hold a lock at a time. You can partially break it with `ReadWriteLock` (shared reads) or fully break it with lock-free structures.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class LockFreeExample {
    // No locks at all: CAS-based thread safety
    private final AtomicInteger transactionCount = new AtomicInteger(0);
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void recordTransaction(String id) {
        cache.put(id, "PROCESSED");
        transactionCount.incrementAndGet();
    }
}
```

### How do you break hold-and-wait?

Acquire all locks at once, or release the current lock before requesting another. `tryLock` enables this pattern.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class BreakHoldAndWait {
    public boolean transferSafely(ReentrantLock lockA, ReentrantLock lockB) {
        if (lockA.tryLock()) {
            try {
                if (lockB.tryLock()) {
                    try {
                        return true; // got both: perform transfer
                    } finally { lockB.unlock(); }
                }
            } finally { lockA.unlock(); }
        }
        return false; // could not get both: no hold-and-wait
    }
}
```

### How do you break no preemption?

`tryLock(timeout)` simulates preemption: the thread gives up voluntarily after a deadline.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class BreakNoPreemption {
    public boolean acquireWithTimeout(ReentrantLock lock) throws InterruptedException {
        if (!lock.tryLock(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            // Could not acquire in 5 seconds: back off voluntarily
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How do you break circular wait?

Assign a global order to all locks and require every thread to acquire them in that order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderedTransfer {
    public void transfer(com.bank.model.Account from,
                         com.bank.model.Account to,
                         BigDecimal amount) {
        // Order by account ID: always lock the lower ID first
        com.bank.model.Account first =
            from.getAccountId().compareTo(to.getAccountId()) < 0 ? from : to;
        com.bank.model.Account second = (first == from) ? to : from;

        synchronized (first.getLock()) {
            synchronized (second.getLock()) {
                from.debit(amount);
                to.credit(amount);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Which condition is easiest to break?

| Condition | How to break | Difficulty | Common? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Circular wait | Lock ordering | Easy | Yes (most common) |
| Hold-and-wait | tryLock (all-or-none) | Medium | Yes (with retry logic) |
| No preemption | tryLock(timeout) | Medium | Yes (safety net) |
| Mutual exclusion | Lock-free structures | Hard | Sometimes |

### Why is breaking one condition enough?

Deadlock requires all four conditions to be true simultaneously. The logical expression is: `deadlock = C1 AND C2 AND C3 AND C4`. If any condition is false, the entire expression is false. Breaking even one condition makes deadlock impossible.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Lock ordering at scale**: in systems with hundreds of lock types, maintain a documented lock hierarchy. If Lock-A is higher in the hierarchy than Lock-B, always acquire A before B.
- **Database deadlock prevention**: `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE ORDER BY primary_key` applies the same lock-ordering principle at the database level.
- **Formal verification**: tools like Java Pathfinder can systematically explore all thread interleavings to prove the absence of deadlocks in critical code.
